---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import DueDateCalculator from '@components/islands/DueDateCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('health', 'due-date-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How is a due date calculated?', answer: 'The most common method is Naegele\'s rule: the estimated due date is 280 days (40 weeks) from the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP). This assumes a regular 28-day cycle with ovulation around day 14.' },
  { question: 'How do I calculate my due date manually?', answer: 'Take the first day of your last period, subtract three months, then add seven days (and a year). For an LMP of March 10, that is December 10 plus seven days — an estimated due date of about December 17 the following year. The calculator does this instantly from the date you enter.' },
  { question: 'How far along am I based on my due date?', answer: 'Your current gestational age is 40 weeks minus the time remaining until your due date. Enter your LMP above and the calculator shows exactly how many weeks and days along you are today, along with your trimester.' },
  { question: 'How accurate is the due date?', answer: 'It is an estimate. Only about 4% of babies are born on their exact due date; most arrive within two weeks either side. An ultrasound dating scan, especially in the first trimester, can refine the estimate.' },
  { question: 'What if my cycle is irregular?', answer: 'Naegele\'s rule assumes a 28-day cycle. If your cycle is consistently longer or shorter, or you know your conception or IVF transfer date, your due date may shift by several days — your healthcare provider can adjust it accordingly.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} reviewedOn="2026-07-18" intro="Estimate your baby's due date from the first day of your last period, and see how far along you are today." faqs={faqs}>
  <DueDateCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How the estimate works</h2>
    <p>Enter the first day of your last menstrual period. Using Naegele's rule, the calculator adds 280 days to estimate your due date, and shows how many weeks and days along you are today, plus your current trimester.</p>

    <h2>The manual calculation</h2>
    <p>You can reproduce the estimate by hand: take the first day of your last period, <strong>subtract three months</strong>, then <strong>add seven days</strong>. For a last period starting March 10, that gives December 10 plus seven days — a due date of about <strong>December 17</strong> the next year. It is the same 40-week window, just counted a shorter way.</p>

    <h2>Remember it's an estimate</h2>
    <p>Very few babies arrive exactly on the due date — it marks the middle of a normal range, and a full-term birth is anything from 37 to 42 weeks. Your provider may refine the date with an early ultrasound, which is generally more accurate than LMP dating if your cycle is irregular. To follow your pregnancy week by week, use the <a href="/health/pregnancy-calculator">pregnancy calculator</a>.</p>
    <p>This is general information, not medical advice. Always follow guidance from your healthcare provider.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
